    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Earthquake ruptures and other large strike-slip faults commonly have segmented traces with overlapping echelon segments. The ability of individual segment to propagate is regulated by mechanical interaction with its neighbors and can lead to localized areas of compression or extension near the segment ends. This results in vertical ground surface displacements that can depend on the sense of fault slip and whether the segments are right- or left-stepping. Basin formation and uplifted mountain ranges can result from the interaction of large crustal faults, while sag ponds and transpressional ridges result on a smaller scale. Calculating surface displacements caused by slip on interacting faults using Poly3D will be a integral part of preparing a mechanically sound fault configuration in the Marmara Sea. Cited as the most susceptible region for the next large Turkish earthquake, the northern half of the Marmara Sea is defined by three fault bound basins. Surface displacement patterns calculated using Poly3D can be compared with the Marmara Sea bathymetry to help constrain faults mapped from seismic interpretation.\r\n\r\nFollowing slip during earthquakes, remote sensing can quickly provide constraints on the region of significant ground surface displacements. In the case of the Izmit earthquake, Scanning Aperture Radar (SAR) images help show that the unobservable submarine portion of the surface rupture is likely to have extended well into the Bay of Izmit. A Poly3D model using the slip from the Izmit earthquake produces a comparable pattern of surface displacement.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
